cff-version: 1.2.0
title: "Combination Cycles: enumeration and catalogue of symmetrical twelve-note interval cycles"
message: "If you use this software or catalogue, please cite it. The underlying theory is set out in the author's 2017 doctoral portfolio, listed first under references."
type: software
authors:
  - family-names: Jeffery
    given-names: Christopher
    orcid: "https://orcid.org/0000-0002-1587-6254"
    affiliation: "University of South Africa"
repository-code: "https://github.com/chrisomatix-glitch/combination-cycles"
url: "https://chrisjeffery.co.za"
abstract: >-
  An engine for enumerating, cataloguing and realising symmetrical twelve-note
  combination cycles: cycles of two or more alternating intervals that traverse
  all twelve pitch classes exactly once before returning to the starting note.
  It implements the theory the author developed across the compositions of his
  2017 doctoral portfolio, where the term "combination cycle" is defined and the
  twelve-note cyclical forms are applied compositionally. It adds a catalogue
  and naming scheme, register placement, serial row forms, and a concordance
  with the independent mathematical enumeration of Berliner et al. (2018).
keywords:
  - music theory
  - interval cycles
  - combination cycles
  - post-tonal theory
  - modes of limited transposition
  - twelve-tone
license: MIT
version: 0.1.0
date-released: 2026-08-08
references:
  # --- The author's prior statement of the theory ---
  - type: thesis
    authors:
      - family-names: Jeffery
        given-names: Christopher
    title: "Portfolio of Original Compositions"
    thesis-type: "DMus (Composition) portfolio"
    institution:
      name: "South African College of Music, University of Cape Town"
    year: 2017
    month: 10
    url: "http://hdl.handle.net/11427/27459"
    notes: >-
      Defines the term "combination cycle" and sets out the twelve-note
      cyclical forms, including the whole-tone constraint on two-interval
      cycles. Applies them compositionally in Movement for Viola and Piano
      (2010), The Outer Edges (2011), String Quartet (2014-15) and the
      orchestral film score De Voortrekkers (2016), the last generated
      entirely from seven such cycles used leitmotivically.

  # --- Earliest public statement. ---
  - type: conference-paper
    authors:
      - family-names: Jeffery
        given-names: Christopher
    title: "Circular symmetry: symmetrical twelve-note modes of alternating intervals, and their place in musical practice"
    conference:
      name: "South African Society for Research in Music (SASRIM), Tshwane University of Technology"
    year: 2012
    notes: "Earliest public presentation of the theory."

  # --- Independent mathematical treatment ---
  - type: article
    authors:
      - family-names: Berliner
        given-names: "Adam H."
      - family-names: Castro
        given-names: David
      - family-names: Merritt
        given-names: Justin
      - family-names: Southard
        given-names: Christopher
    title: "Expanded interval cycles"
    journal: "Journal of Mathematics and Music"
    volume: 12
    issue: 1
    start: 21
    end: 33
    year: 2018
    doi: "10.1080/17459737.2018.1453950"
    notes: >-
      Independent and more rigorous mathematical development, giving necessary
      and sufficient conditions and complete enumeration tables. Does not
      address inversional distinctions, compositional application, or the
      relationship to Messiaen's modes of limited transposition.
